---
{state.status === "retired" ? <StateEvent view={state} /> : <BaseLayout title={`${event.title} | The Catch`} current="Events">
  <div slot="locator" class="locator"><a href="/events/">Series</a><a href="/events/mail-voting/">Mail voting</a><span>September 2026</span></div>
  <div class="content">
    <main class="story" id="story-root">
      <p class="story-kicker" data-layer="narrative">Supreme Court &middot; September 6, 2026</p>
      <h1 data-layer="fact">{event.title}</h1>
      <p class="story-dek" data-layer="fact">{event.dek}</p>
      <ReadingModes />

      <nav class="story-toc" aria-label="In this story" data-layer="fact">
        <details class="toc-disclosure" open><summary><span>In this story</span><span class="toc-current" aria-hidden="true"></span></summary>
        <ol>
          <li><a href="#what-happened">What happened</a></li>
          <li><a href="#why-blocked">Why the judge blocked it</a></li>
          <li><a href="#the-catch">The catch</a></li>
          <li><a href="#where-this-sits">Where this sits</a></li>
          <li><a href="#who-feels-it">Who feels it</a></li>
          <li><a href="#outlets">What the coverage got right, and what it got wrong</a></li>
          <li><a href="#claim-check">Claim checked against the record</a></li>
          <li><a href="#what-happened-next">What happened next</a></li>
          <li><a href="#unknowns">What we do not know yet</a></li>
          <li><a href="#records">The records</a></li>
        </ol>
      </details></nav>
      <div class="kpi-strip" data-layer="fact">
        {event.kpis.map((k) => (
          <div class="kpi">
            <span class="kpi-value">{k.value}<small>{k.unit ? ` ${k.unit}` : ""}</small></span>
            <span class="kpi-label">{k.label}</span>
          </div>
        ))}
      </div>

      <aside class="three-things" data-layer="fact">
        <p class="tt-label">Three things to know</p>
        <ol>
          <li>On Sunday, September 6, Solicitor General D. John Sauer filed application 26A305. An application is a request to a justice to freeze a lower-court order while an appeal continues. This one asks the Supreme Court to stay, meaning pause, a September 4 injunction from U.S. District Judge Indira Talwani in Boston.<Cite s="scotus-26a305-docket" passage="No. 26A305" /></li>
          <li>Talwani's injunction forbids the Postal Service from carrying out named mandatory sections of its Ballot Mail for Federal Elections rule, issued and effective August 21 and published August 26, for elections on or before November 3. It dissolves her August 27 temporary restraining order, a 14-day pause that the new order replaces.<Cite s="talwani-pi-2026-09-04" passage="elections occurring before or on November 3" /><Cite s="usps-final-rule" passage="Effective August 21, 2026" /></li>
          <li>The same Sunday Sauer withdrew application 26A297, filed Thursday against that temporary order. The word "another" in the coverage is this third trip to the Court. Application 26A124, filed July 27, was granted August 24.<Cite s="scotus-26a297-withdrawal" passage="the United States withdraws its September 3 stay application" /><Cite s="scotus-26a124-docket" passage="Application (26A124) granted by the Court." /></li>
        </ol>
      </aside>
      <p class="railed rail-note" data-layer="fact">Story updated {event.updated}.</p>

      <div data-layer="proof" class="event-record-context">
        <p class="sources-line">This story rests on Executive Order 14399 as published in the Federal Register, the Postal Service's final rule (issued and effective August 21, published August 26), the Privacy Act notices for system USPS 820.225, Judge Talwani's September 4 injunction, the three Supreme Court applications and their dockets, Sauer's withdrawal letter, 2 U.S.C. 7, 52 U.S.C. 20302, Senator Blumenthal's September 1 whistleblower release, the Postal Service's September 1 portal statement, and coverage checked against those records. The days on this page are counted from those dates. The figures come from the official Federal Register PDFs.</p>
      </div>

      <section id="what-happened">
        <p class="sec-kicker" data-layer="narrative">The order first, then the rule, then the Court</p>
        <h2 data-layer="fact">What happened</h2>

        <p data-layer="narrative">On March 31, 2026, President Donald J. Trump signed Executive Order 14399, "Ensuring Citizenship Verification and Integrity in Federal Elections." Section 3 of that order directed the Postmaster General to start a rulemaking on mail-in and absentee ballots, including a proposed line that the Postal Service "shall not transmit" those ballots from anyone not enrolled on a state-specific list.<Cite s="eo-14399" passage="the USPS shall not transmit mail-" /></p>

        <p data-layer="narrative">The rule did not go straight from the order to the Federal Register. On June 2 the Postal Service published a proposed rule; more than 200,000 comments came in. On June 25 Talwani ruled for 23 states and the District of Columbia in their suit against the order, and on July 7 she entered a final judgment forbidding the named agencies from carrying out Sections 2 and 3, but only for those plaintiffs and only for elections through November 3. On July 25 the First Circuit refused to pause that judgment while the government appealed. Its order said the government and twelve intervening states argued "only that the Plaintiff States lack standing to bring their claims and that those claims are not ripe," and closed: "We deny their motions."<Cite s="talwani-pi-2026-09-04" passage="On June 2, 2026, the USPS issued its proposed rule." /><Cite s="ca1-stay-denial-2026-07-25" passage="We deny their motions." /></p>

        <p data-layer="narrative">On August 11, in the separate case brought by the League of Women Voters, Talwani entered a nationwide preliminary injunction. It barred the Postal Service defendants "from implementing, giving effect to, or enforcing Section 3" of the order for the November 3 election or any earlier one, and from "initiating or completing rulemaking" on the regulations the order described.<Cite s="talwani-pi-2026-09-04" passage="initiating or completing" /></p>

        <p data-layer="narrative">On August 21 the Postal Service issued a final rule, Ballot Mail for Federal Elections, and made it effective that day. The rule was published in the Federal Register on August 26. It amends the Domestic Mail Manual. It requires official Election Mail logos, automation-compatible envelopes, unique Intelligent Mail barcodes, and an online Federal Ballot Mail Portal where election officials enroll the name, address, and barcodes of people they intend to send a ballot. Both injunctions were still in force that Friday evening. The rule's own text said the Postal Service would not implement it for the 2026 election "unless and until the government obtains relief from those injunctions."<Cite s="usps-final-rule" passage="Effective August 21, 2026" /><Cite s="talwani-enforcement-2026-08-25" passage="unless and until the government obtains relief from those injunctions" /></p>

        <p data-layer="narrative">Four days later Talwani ruled that issuing the rule broke her August 11 order. The League of Women Voters had moved to enforce the injunction. The government answered that the rule would not be enforced for 2026 unless the injunctions were lifted. Talwani wrote that those arguments "ignore the court's explicit directive not to" initiate or complete rulemaking, and that "the court finds that Defendants violated the preliminary injunction in this case." She imposed "no remedy other than identifying the violation."<Cite s="talwani-enforcement-2026-08-25" passage="no remedy other than identifying the violation" /></p>

        <p data-layer="narrative">Outbound mailings presented by election offices that do not comply "will not be accepted" and "will be returned" to the "authorized ballot mailer." Section 705.24.5 "does not apply to Return Federal Ballot Mail." Return envelopes still have design rules; the batch nonacceptance screen is the outbound procedure.<Cite s="usps-final-rule" passage="does not apply to Return Federal Ballot Mail" /></p>

        <p data-layer="narrative">The rule states an "express exclusion of UOCAVA ballots" from its scope. The Postal Service wrote that those voters "will experience no change" if the exemption is applied as written. UOCAVA covers uniformed and overseas voters under 52 U.S.C. 20302.<Cite s="usps-final-rule" passage="express exclusion of UOCAVA ballots" /><Cite s="uocava-52usc20302" passage="not later than 45 days before the election" /></p>

        <p data-layer="narrative">The portal sits on a federal Privacy Act system, "USPS 820.225 Federal Ballot Mail." The July 17 notice places it at "USPS Headquarters, Supplier cloud" computing environment. The first category of records is "Mail in voter information: Name," address, unique Intelligent Mail barcode numbers, and originating election-office state. Records are retrievable by name, address, issuing state, and barcode, and "Records are retained for 5 years." A stated purpose is to help determine adherence to federal law and facilitate law-enforcement efforts. State and local officials load the rows. The system is not a citizenship file. The Postal Service's September 1 response to comments said the system is effective with that publication.<Cite s="usps-sorn-820-225" passage="Records are retained for 5 years." /><Cite s="usps-sorn-response-2026-09-01" passage="effective with this publication of the" /></p>

        <p data-layer="narrative">On August 24 the Supreme Court granted application 26A124, staying the July judgment for the states. A stay is an order that pauses another court's order. The unsigned opinion said the government was likely to win its argument that the district court lacked jurisdiction: "Two related doctrines of justiciability block the States' suit," standing and ripeness, and the Court took "each challenged provision of the Order separately." On Section 3, the Postal Service directive, it held that the district court had relied on "a string of speculations" about a rule that had not yet issued, and wrote that "Federal courts review final rules, not proposed rules." The intervenor states' own application, 26A139, was "denied as moot" the same day. The opinion added that the disposition "does not mean that any measure taken by the Government to implement the Order will necessarily be lawful."<Cite s="scotus-26a124-opinion" passage="Two related doctrines of justiciability block the States" /><Cite s="scotus-26a124-opinion" passage="Federal courts review final rules, not proposed rules" /><Cite s="scotus-26a124-opinion" passage="any measure taken by the Government to implement" /><Cite s="scotus-26a139-docket" passage="denied as moot" /></p>

        <p data-layer="narrative">On August 27 Talwani entered a temporary restraining order against mandatory parts of the new rule. On September 3 Sauer asked the Court, in application 26A297, to stay that order. The docket shows the application was submitted to Justice Jackson. She set a response for September 8. The emergency docket is the Court's fast track for those requests, usually without a full argument.<Cite s="scotus-26a297-docket" passage="submitted to Justice Jackson" /></p>

        <p data-layer="narrative">On September 4 Talwani issued a preliminary injunction. A preliminary injunction is a court order that forbids someone from doing something while a lawsuit continues. This one names specific Domestic Mail Manual sections and applies to elections on or before November 3. She dissolved the August 27 temporary order the same day. These holdings are preliminary findings for that order, not a final judgment on the merits.<Cite s="talwani-pi-2026-09-04" passage="The Temporary Restraining Order" /></p>

        <h3 id="why-blocked" data-layer="fact">Why the judge blocked it</h3>

        <p data-layer="narrative">Talwani wrote under "Likelihood of Success on the Merits" that the plaintiffs had established a substantial likelihood of success on their Elections Clause claim. She found the rule is an election rule that regulates the time, place, and manner of elections, and that it is unconstitutional where Congress has not authorized such a rule under the Elections Clause.<Cite s="talwani-pi-2026-09-04" passage="Likelihood of Success on the Merits" /></p>

        <p data-layer="narrative">She separately found the rule likely ultra vires, meaning beyond the agency's statutory authority: Congress has not authorized the Postal Service to make this election rule, and Title 39 does not grant that power.<Cite s="talwani-pi-2026-09-04" passage="The Final Rule is Also Ultra Vires" /></p>

        <p data-layer="narrative">She also found the rule likely creates a new category of nonmailable material, federal election mail, contrary to Title 39's scheme for what Congress has listed as nonmailable.<Cite s="talwani-pi-2026-09-04" passage="new category of nonmailable materials" /></p>

        <p data-layer="narrative">The government contests those findings. In application 26A305, Sauer writes that "The Rule is a lawful and modest exercise" of rulemaking authority Congress delegated to the Postal Service, that "it regulates the U.S. Mail, not federal elections," and that "it does not regulate state administration of elections."<Cite s="scotus-26a305-application" passage="The Rule is a lawful and modest exercise" /></p>

        <p data-layer="narrative">On September 6 Sauer wrote the clerk that the United States was withdrawing 26A297, and he filed 26A305 against the new injunction. Jackson's order on that docket set any response for 4 p.m. Eastern on September 9. Sunday is {computed.daysFilingToElection} days before November 3, the federal election day named in the injunction and in 2 U.S.C. 7 as the Tuesday after the first Monday in November of an even-numbered year.<Cite s="scotus-26a305-docket" passage="4 p.m. (EDT) on September 9, 2026." /><Cite s="usc-2-7" passage="Tuesday next after the 1st Monday in November" /></p>

        <figure class="story-figure" data-layer="fact">
          <figcaption><strong>From the order to the election</strong><span>Dated steps in the filings and dockets.</span></figcaption>
          <DecisionTimeline entries={timeline} />
          <p class="chart-source">Sunday's application is the solicitor general's third stay request in this fight, and the fourth counting the intervenor states' 26A139, which the Court denied as moot on August 24.<Cite s="scotus-26a305-docket" passage="No. 26A305" /><Cite s="scotus-26a139-docket" passage="denied as moot by the Court" /></p>
        </figure>

        <figure class="story-figure" data-layer="fact">
          <figcaption><strong>The four court steps Sunday's application sits on</strong><span>Temporary order, Thursday's stay request, the injunction, Sunday's stay request.</span></figcaption>
          <DecisionTimeline entries={courtSteps} />
          <p class="chart-source">26A297 was withdrawn the day 26A305 was docketed.<Cite s="scotus-26a297-docket" passage="Application (26A297) withdrawn." /></p>
        </figure>

        <figure class="story-figure" data-layer="fact">
          <figcaption><strong>The enjoined Domestic Mail Manual sections</strong><span>What each named section of the August 26 rule requires, in the rule's own terms.</span></figcaption>
          <DataTable columns={["Section", "What it requires"]} rows={dmmRows} highlightColumn={0} />
          <p class="chart-source">The September 4 injunction names these sections and the first two sentences of 705.24.5.2. It does not enjoin 705.24.5.3(d). Separate from the injunction, section 705.24.5 "does not apply to Return Federal Ballot Mail."<Cite s="talwani-pi-2026-09-04" passage="DMM Section 705.24.5.3(a), (b), (c)" /><Cite s="usps-final-rule" passage="does not apply to Return Federal Ballot Mail" /></p>
        </figure>

        <QuoteCard when="September 6, 2026" where="Application 26A305" label="Solicitor General D. John Sauer">
          <p>Ballots have already begun to be mailed in North Carolina, and more States will begin the mailing process as the injunction remains in place.<Cite s="scotus-26a305-application" passage="Ballots have already begun to be mailed in North Carolina" /></p>
        </QuoteCard>

        <QuoteCard when="September 4, 2026" where="Memorandum and order, District of Massachusetts" label="Judge Indira Talwani">
          <p>... the court finds, on one side, that immediate implementation of the Final Rule issued less than seventy days before the November 2026 election threatens disenfranchisement of millions of United States citizens who seek to vote by mail.<Cite s="talwani-pi-2026-09-04" passage="the court finds, on one side," /></p>
        </QuoteCard>

        <QuoteCard when="September 4, 2026" where="Memorandum and order, District of Massachusetts" label="Judge Indira Talwani">
          <p>... fraudulent mail voting to support the rushed implementation of the Final Rule.<Cite s="talwani-pi-2026-09-04" passage="fraudulent mail voting to support the rushed implementation" /></p>
        </QuoteCard>

        <QuoteCard when="August 24, 2026" where="Dissent in Trump v. California, 26A124" label="Justice Ketanji Brown Jackson">
          <p>It also lets another shoe drop in the Kafkaesque nightmare that our precedents have been steadily creating for certain plaintiffs who seek to bring election-related challenges.<Cite s="scotus-26a124-opinion" passage="Kafkaesque" /></p>
        </QuoteCard>

        <QuoteCard when="September 8, 2026" where="Amicus brief of bipartisan current and former members of Congress, 26A305" label="Heading I">
          <p>THE ORDER AND FINAL RULE USURP CONGRESS’S CONSTITUTIONAL ROLE AS THE ULTIMATE AUTHORITY ON FEDERAL-ELECTION ADMINISTRATION.<Cite s="scotus-26a305-congress-amicus" passage="THE ORDER AND FINAL RULE USURP CONGRESS" /></p>
        </QuoteCard>

        <SourcedBlock source="scotus-26a305-application" kind="record" detail>
          <p>The Sunday application asks the Court to stay the September 4 injunction pending appeal to the First Circuit and any later petition, and it asks for an immediate administrative stay, a short pause while the justices decide the full request. Sauer writes that the rule imposes "modest envelope-design and addressee-information requirements", and that states remain responsible for who may vote by mail.<Cite s="scotus-26a305-application" passage="addressee-information requirements" /></p></SourcedBlock>

        <SourcedBlock source="talwani-pi-2026-09-04" kind="record" detail>
          <p>The injunction names Domestic Mail Manual sections 705.24.3.1, 705.24.3.2, 705.24.4.1, 705.24.4.2, 705.24.5.1, the first two sentences of 705.24.5.2, and 705.24.5.3(a), (b), and (c). Those are the envelope standards, portal registration and enrollment, and the noncompliance rules. It does not bar states from complying voluntarily. It does not enjoin the rule for elections after November 3.<Cite s="talwani-pi-2026-09-04" passage="DMM Section 705.24.5.3(a), (b), (c)" /></p></SourcedBlock>
      </section>

      <section id="the-catch">
        <aside class="the-catch" data-layer="fact">
          <p class="tt-label">The catch</p>
          <ol>
            <li>
              <span class="catch-tag">Told versus record</span>
              <span class="catch-lead">The block is of the rule, not the order.</span>
              The Associated Press, PBS NewsHour, and CNBC wrote that Talwani extended an order against the Postal Service "enforcing President Donald Trump's executive order." CBS wrote "enforcing President Trump's executive order." The September 4 injunction names sections of the Postal Service rule issued August 21 and published August 26. The Court's August 24 stay had already paused her earlier injunction of the executive order itself. Why it matters: a reader of those dispatches can think the justices are being asked to revive the March order, when the fight before the Court is the Postal Service's published rule.<Cite s="ap-2026-09-06" passage="enforcing President Donald Trump" /><Cite s="cbs-2026-09-06" passage="enforcing President Trump's executive order" /><Cite s="talwani-pi-2026-09-04" passage="Ballot Mail for Federal Elections" />
              <a class="catch-jump" href="#claim-check">Claim check</a>
            </li>
            <li>
              <span class="catch-tag">Told versus record</span>
              <span class="catch-lead">Citizenship lists are a different paper.</span>
              The Hill wrote that Sauer asked the justices to "permit the administration to bar the U.S. Postal Service from sending mail-in ballots to residents of states that have not submitted a list of eligible voters based on citizenship data." The Sunday application asks the Court to stay the September 4 injunction of the Postal Service rule. The rule's portal fields are name, address, two barcodes, and originating state. The citizenship list sits in section 2 of the executive order, a Homeland Security task, not in the Postal Service rule's enrollment form. Why it matters: the injunction the Court is being asked to lift reaches envelope standards, portal registration and enrollment, outbound verification, and the nonacceptance clauses, not a citizenship roll, and it is a Postal Service rule, not an order that the administration bar the Postal Service.<Cite s="hill-2026-09-06" passage="permit the administration to bar the U.S. Postal Service" /><Cite s="eo-14399" passage="State Citizenship List" />
              <a class="catch-jump" href="#claim-check">Claim check</a>
            </li>
            <li>
              <span class="catch-tag">Left out</span>
              <span class="catch-lead">Thursday's application is closed.</span>
              Coverage of Sunday's filing called it "another" appeal. The docket for 26A297, the Thursday filing, shows it withdrawn the same day 26A305 was docketed. Jackson's Sunday order is a response deadline of 4 p.m. on September 9. Why it matters: Sunday's application is 26A305; the Thursday application is not pending.<Cite s="scotus-26a297-docket" passage="Application (26A297) withdrawn." /><Cite s="scotus-26a305-docket" passage="4 p.m. (EDT) on September 9, 2026." />
              <a class="catch-jump" href="#where-this-sits">Where this sits</a>
            </li>
            <li>
              <span class="catch-tag">Left out</span>
              <span class="catch-lead">The rule was issued against an injunction, the judge found.</span>
              Sunday's application presents the August 21 rule as an ordinary final rule. On August 25 Talwani found that completing it violated her August 11 order, which had barred "initiating or completing rulemaking." She imposed no remedy beyond the finding. That order is not mentioned in the application.<Cite s="talwani-enforcement-2026-08-25" passage="court finds that Defendants violated the preliminary injunction in this case." /><Cite s="talwani-pi-2026-09-04" passage="initiating or completing" />
              <a class="catch-jump" href="#what-happened">What happened</a>
            </li>
            <li>
              <span class="catch-tag">Who pays</span>
              <span class="catch-lead">Mail voters in the plaintiff states.</span>
              The September 4 order binds the Postal Service defendants for elections on or before November 3. It does not limit that command to the plaintiff states. Sauer told the Court North Carolina had already begun mailing, Alabama would on September 9, and at least five states the week of September 13. If the stay is granted, election offices sending non-UOCAVA outbound ballots would face the portal and envelope rules while some of those mailings are already moving. Why it matters: the next order lands on those offices and the voters waiting on outbound ballots, not only on voters in the states that sued, and not on UOCAVA ballots the rule excludes.<Cite s="scotus-26a305-application" passage="Ballots have already begun to be mailed in North Carolina" /><Cite s="talwani-pi-2026-09-04" passage="elections occurring before or on November 3" />
              <a class="catch-jump" href="#who-feels-it">Who feels it</a>
            </li>
          </ol>
          <p class="catch-minor">Smaller slips: The Associated Press Sunday story spells Biden as "Joe Bden." NBC's "federal database" line is treated under the outlet cards, where it checks out against the Privacy Act notice.<Cite s="ap-2026-09-06" passage="Joe Bden" /><Cite s="nbc-2026-09-06" passage="not included in a federal database" /></p>
        </aside>
      </section>

      <section id="where-this-sits">
        <p class="sec-kicker" data-layer="narrative">Four stay applications, one election date</p>
        <h2 data-layer="fact">Where this sits</h2>

        <figure class="story-figure" data-layer="fact">
          <figcaption><strong>Days remaining to November 3, from each dated step</strong><span>Counted from the dates in the filings to the election named in 2 U.S.C. 7 and in the injunction.</span></figcaption>
          <BarChart bars={daysBars} ariaLabel="Days from each dated step to November 3, 2026" highlightLast={true} />
          <p class="chart-source">{computed.daysEffectiveToElection} days run from the rule's August 21 effective date to November 3. {computed.daysRuleToElection} days run from August 26 publication to that election. Sunday's filing is {computed.daysFilingToElection} days before the election. September 19 is the UOCAVA statutory mailing date, {computed.daysFilingToUocava} days after the filing; the rule excludes UOCAVA ballots, so that date is context, not a deadline under this rule.<Cite s="usps-final-rule" passage="Effective August 21, 2026" /><Cite s="uocava-52usc20302" passage="the request is received at least 45 days before an election" /></p>
          <p class="chart-vintage" data-layer="proof">Day counts are calendar days between those dates at UTC midnight. {computed.daysEffectiveToElection} is November 3 minus August 21. {computed.daysRuleToElection} is November 3 minus August 26. {computed.daysFilingToElection} is November 3 minus September 6. {computed.uocavaDaysBeforeElection} is November 3 minus September 19.</p>
        </figure>

        <figure class="story-figure" data-layer="fact">
          <figcaption><strong>The three stay applications</strong><span>From the Supreme Court dockets and Sauer's withdrawal letter.</span></figcaption>
          <DataTable columns={["Docket", "Filed", "Relief asked", "What the Court did"]} rows={applicationsTable} highlightColumn={0} />
          <p class="chart-source">26A139, a companion application by Alabama and other states in the first round, was denied as moot when 26A124 was granted.<Cite s="scotus-26a124-docket" passage="Application (26A124) granted by the Court." /></p>
        </figure>

        <p data-layer="narrative">The Sunday application's caption lists {computed.plaintiffStates} states, the District of Columbia, and Josh Shapiro, Governor of Pennsylvania, as plaintiffs. {computed.intervenorStates} states intervened on the government's side. Washington Attorney General Nick Brown's September 4 release said "25 states have secured a" preliminary injunction. That is the same coalition: Brown names 23 states, including Washington, plus the District of Columbia and Pennsylvania's governor. "25 states" is a loose label because the District of Columbia is not a state.<Cite s="scotus-26a305-application" passage="Josh Shapiro, Governor of Pennsylvania" /><Cite s="brown-2026-09-04" passage="25 states have secured a" /></p>

        <p data-layer="narrative">August 24 was a win on procedure, not on the rule. The Court stayed the judgment against the executive order on jurisdiction, provision by provision. For the Postal Service section it said the states' injury rested on a "string of speculations" about a rule that had not issued; the other sections fell on standing. The Postal Service had already issued that rule and made it effective on August 21, {computed.daysEffectiveToStay} days before the opinion. The rule reached the Federal Register {computed.daysStayToRule} days after the opinion, on August 26. Talwani then enjoined named sections of that rule. Sunday's application argues that the new injunction is "materially identical" to the August 27 temporary order and that the Postal Service is regulating mail, not elections.<Cite s="scotus-26a124-opinion" passage="string of speculations" /><Cite s="scotus-26a305-application" passage="materially identical" /></p>

        <p data-layer="narrative">The dates leave little room. The public record of this fight starts with the March 31 order and runs through a June proposed rule, a July judgment, and two August injunctions before the rule issued. Sunday's stay request was filed {computed.daysFilingToElection} days before the election the injunction names, and {computed.daysFilingToJackson} days before Jackson's response deadline. September 19 is the UOCAVA statutory mailing date, {computed.daysFilingToUocava} days after the filing; the rule's "express exclusion of UOCAVA ballots" means that date is not a deadline this rule imposes.<Cite s="scotus-26a305-docket" passage="4 p.m. (EDT) on September 9, 2026." /><Cite s="usps-final-rule" passage="express exclusion of UOCAVA ballots" /></p>

        <SourcedBlock source="scotus-26a305-application" kind="record" detail>
          <p>Plaintiff states named in the Sunday application: {plaintiffs.join(", ")}. Also {plaintiffOther.join("; ")}. Intervenors: {intervenors.join(", ")}. Organization plaintiffs: {orgPlaintiffs.join("; ")}.<Cite s="scotus-26a305-application" passage="League of Women Voters of Massachusetts" /></p></SourcedBlock>
      </section>

      <section id="who-feels-it">
        <p class="sec-kicker" data-layer="narrative">Election offices, outbound ballots, and the next few days</p>
        <h2 data-layer="fact">Who feels it</h2>

        <p data-layer="narrative">The immediate dispute is narrower than mail voting as a whole. The rule reaches outbound ballot mailings that election offices present to the Postal Service for a general, special, or runoff election for federal office. Its definition "does not include primary elections." UOCAVA ballots are excluded. The verification section "does not apply to Return Federal Ballot Mail," so the envelope a voter sends back is outside it, and state-only elections are not federal elections under the rule. The injunction is what keeps the Postal Service from treating noncompliant outbound mailings as unaccepted for elections on or before November 3. If the Court stays that injunction, offices sending those outbound ballots would have to meet the envelope and portal rules while some of those mailings are already moving. Sauer's own line is that once envelopes "enter the mailstream, there is no retrieving them."<Cite s="scotus-26a305-application" passage="there is no retrieving them" /><Cite s="usps-final-rule" passage="include primary elections." /><Cite s="usps-final-rule" passage="does not apply to Return Federal Ballot Mail" /></p>

        <p data-layer="narrative"><strong>What the whistleblower alleged.</strong> An anonymous USPS whistleblower, in a disclosure Senator Richard Blumenthal released on September 1, said the portal work was rushed and siloed, that the project parts "have not undergone meaningful testing," and that USPS adopted a "zero-percent failure threshold" under which one failed barcode could return an entire batch: "if a barcode scanner fails to correctly read one barcode in a batch, the entire batch is rejected and returned to the state." Those are allegations, not established facts.<Cite s="blumenthal-whistleblower-2026-09-01" passage="zero-percent failure threshold" /></p>

        <p data-layer="narrative"><strong>What a second account said.</strong> ProPublica reported on September 2, from a person familiar with the internal meetings, that "Some in the meetings recommended trying to limit harm to voters by not rejecting the entire batch." Two people who attended a committee meeting told ProPublica that, on the example of Maricopa County's initial mailing, "USPS officials confirmed it was possible that one ballot with a bad barcode or wrong voter information within the more than 2 million ballots could delay the entire mailing." That account is separate from the whistleblower's and is also anonymous.<Cite s="propublica-2026-09-02" passage="not rejecting the entire batch" /><Cite s="propublica-2026-09-02" passage="could delay the entire mailing" /></p>

        <p data-layer="narrative"><strong>What the whistleblower's lawyers told the Court.</strong> On September 8 Whistleblower Aid filed an amicus brief on the 26A305 docket. It describes two chokepoints: a portal check before a batch reaches a post office, and a counter check where clerks scan a sample. It says "the verification standard is zero percent failure," that "the unit of rejection is the batch, not the ballot," and that the systems as designed "reject entire batches of ballots if they find even a single mismatch." The sampling schedule it quotes from the disclosure, still provisional a week before the planned September 1 launch, is in the table below.<Cite s="scotus-26a305-whistleblower-aid-amicus" passage="the verification standard is zero percent failure" /><Cite s="scotus-26a305-whistleblower-aid-amicus" passage="the unit of rejection is the batch, not the ballot" /><Cite s="scotus-26a305-whistleblower-aid-amicus" passage="reject entire batches of ballots if they find even a single mismatch" /></p>

        <figure class="story-figure" data-layer="fact">
          <figcaption><strong>Pieces scanned per batch, per the disclosure</strong><span>As quoted in the Whistleblower Aid brief. Provisional numbers; the rule itself does not mention sampling.</span></figcaption>
          <DataTable columns={["Batch size", "Pieces sampled"]} rows={sampleRows} highlightColumn={0} />
          <p class="chart-vintage" data-layer="proof">From page 11 of the brief: "15 sampled pieces from a batch under 1,000; 350 from a batch of 1,000 to 10,000; and 400 from a batch above 10,000." The brief adds that the rule "does not mention sampling, though it assumes it." We hold the brief, not the disclosure's own text.<Cite s="scotus-26a305-whistleblower-aid-amicus" passage="15 sampled pieces from a batch under 1,000" /><Cite s="scotus-26a305-whistleblower-aid-amicus" passage="does not mention sampling, though it assumes it" /></p>
        </figure>

        <p data-layer="narrative"><strong>What the Postal Service said.</strong> On September 1 the Postal Service said it had "spent months developing a U.S. Federal Ballot Mail Portal," that the work "has at all times been conducted in a manner consistent with court orders," that it was "finalizing the portal" for officials who "voluntarily wish to familiarize themselves," and that "verification procedures prior to acceptance are not being performed."<Cite s="usps-portal-statement-2026-09-01" passage="spent months developing a U.S. Federal Ballot Mail Portal" /></p>

        <p data-layer="narrative"><strong>What the Postmaster General said.</strong> On September 4 David Steiner answered the whistleblower directly. He wrote that the state-provided list "is merely a list of voters that a State says are receiving a ballot through the mail," that "Testing and validation have been continuous throughout the development lifecycle," that "our standards for quality, security, and system integrity were not compromised," and that "our process requires that deployment be delayed whenever significant defects or security vulnerabilities are identified." He did not say what was tested or publish results.<Cite s="usps-steiner-statement-2026-09-04" passage="Testing and validation have been continuous throughout the development lifecycle" /><Cite s="usps-steiner-statement-2026-09-04" passage="our standards for quality, security, and system integrity were not compromised" /></p>

        <p data-layer="narrative"><strong>What would settle it.</strong> Test reports, a defect list, the sampling specification, or a production record. Neither side has put any of those on a docket we hold. Until one does, the readiness of the portal is a contested claim on both sides, not a fact.<Cite s="scotus-26a305-whistleblower-aid-amicus" passage="still inoperable" /><Cite s="usps-steiner-statement-2026-09-04" passage="We stand behind both the rigor of our process and the resulting system." /></p>

        <p data-layer="narrative"><strong>What the court found.</strong> Talwani wrote that the "Portal is not currently operational" and that the "absence of an appeals or escalation process" supported the states' irreparable-harm claim. The compressed approval and enrollment process, she found, supported blocking mandatory use through November 3.<Cite s="talwani-pi-2026-09-04" passage="Portal is not currently operational" /></p>

        <ol class="dated-list" data-layer="fact">
          <li><strong>September 4:</strong> North Carolina, Sauer told the Court, had already begun mailing ballots. NPR, writing the day before, said the state had to start mailing absentee ballots on Friday.<Cite s="scotus-26a305-application" passage="Ballots have already begun to be mailed in North Carolina" /><Cite s="npr-2026-09-03" passage="North Carolina has to start mailing out its absentee ballots on Friday" /></li>
          <li><strong>September 9, 4 p.m. Eastern:</strong> Jackson's deadline for a response to 26A305. The same calendar day is the Alabama mailing date Sauer named.<Cite s="scotus-26a305-docket" passage="4 p.m. (EDT) on September 9, 2026." /></li>
          <li><strong>Week of September 13:</strong> "at least five States," Sauer wrote, begin mailing.<Cite s="scotus-26a305-application" passage="at least five States the week of September 13" /></li>
          <li><strong>November 3:</strong> federal election day, {computed.daysFilingToElection} days after the filing, {computed.daysPiToElection} days after the injunction.<Cite s="usc-2-7" passage="Tuesday next after the 1st Monday in November" /></li>
        </ol>

        <figure class="story-figure" data-layer="fact">
          <figcaption><strong>Mailing dates Sauer named</strong><span>From the September 6 application, and the overseas-ballot date in the injunction record.</span></figcaption>
          <DataTable columns={["Who", "When"]} rows={mailingRows} highlightColumn={0} />
          <p class="chart-source">Sauer wrote that ballots had already begun to be mailed in North Carolina, with Alabama on September 9 and at least five states the week of September 13. The UOCAVA row is the statutory date; the rule's "express exclusion of UOCAVA ballots" keeps that date off the rule's calendar.<Cite s="scotus-26a305-application" passage="at least five States the week of September 13" /><Cite s="usps-final-rule" passage="express exclusion of UOCAVA ballots" /></p>
        </figure>

        <p data-layer="narrative"><strong>How many ballots.</strong> Talwani's order says "millions" without a national count, and none exists yet for 2026. Two things do exist. The states' own forecasts are in the injunction record: California expects to mail "more than 23 million" ballots, Washington "approximately 5.1 million," Colorado "more than 4,000,000," Oregon "approximately 3 million." The order also says the rule would reach "10,000 different election jurisdictions" and that printing new envelopes takes "four to sixteen weeks." For scale, the last midterm's federal survey counted {computed.eavsTransmitted2022.toLocaleString("en-US")} mail ballots sent in 2022 and {computed.eavsReturned2022.toLocaleString("en-US")} returned. That is a baseline from a different year, not a forecast for this one.<Cite s="talwani-pi-2026-09-04" passage="county election officials will mail more than 23" /><Cite s="talwani-pi-2026-09-04" passage="10,000 different election jurisdictions" /><Cite s="talwani-pi-2026-09-04" passage="four to sixteen weeks" /><Cite s="eavs-2022-report" passage="States reported transmitting 61,460,139 mail ballots" /></p>

        <figure class="story-figure" data-layer="fact">
          <figcaption><strong>What eight states told the court they will mail</strong><span>Forecasts from state declarations quoted in the September 4 order. Largest first.</span></figcaption>
          <DataTable columns={["State", "Ballots to be mailed, 2026 general"]} rows={stateEstimateRows} highlightColumn={0} />
          <p class="chart-vintage" data-layer="proof">Pages 32 and 33 of the order, footnote 37, quoting the declarations filed in the states' second case. Arizona's figure includes UOCAVA voters, which the rule excludes. The 2022 survey figures are from page 22 of the Election Assistance Commission report: {computed.eavsReturnRatePct} percent of ballots sent were returned.<Cite s="talwani-pi-2026-09-04" passage="Washington anticipates mailing ballots to approximately 5.1 million voters" /><Cite s="eavs-2022-report" passage="which 36,683,450 were returned by voters" /></p>
        </figure>

        <p data-layer="narrative">Election offices that use the mail would have to reprint envelopes, load the portal, and certify designs if the stay is granted. Talwani wrote that the record includes "no evidence relating to" fraudulent mail voting to support rushing the rule. The injunction leaves voluntary compliance open.<Cite s="talwani-pi-2026-09-04" passage="no evidence relating to" /></p>
      </section>

      <section id="outlets">
        <p class="sec-kicker" data-layer="narrative">Checked against the filings</p>
        <h2 data-layer="fact">What the coverage got right, and what it got wrong</h2>
        <p data-layer="narrative">Sunday's story ran on the Associated Press wire and on PBS and CNBC in that form, on CBS with a shorter "President Trump" line, and in separate writes at NBC and The Hill. Thursday's application had its own Hill, CBS, and NPR stories. The errors below are the ones the filings contradict.<Cite s="ap-2026-09-06" passage="The appeal marks the third time" /><Cite s="cbs-2026-09-06" passage="enforcing President Trump's executive order" /></p>
        <p data-layer="fact">Six outlet cards: three mislabeled, two that check out, one consistent.</p>

        <div data-layer="fact">
          <OutletCheck outlet="The Hill" claim={`wrote on Sunday that Sauer asked the justices to &ldquo;permit the administration to bar the U.S. Postal Service from sending mail-in ballots to residents of states that have not submitted a list of eligible voters based on citizenship data.&rdquo;`} verdict="The Sunday application asks the Court to stay the September 4 injunction of the Postal Service rule. The portal fields in the rule are name, address, barcodes, and originating state. The citizenship list is section 2 of the executive order. The 40-page count and the withdrawal of Thursday's application check out. Talwani's order covers elections on or before November 3; The Hill also wrote that she halted the plan 'while lower courts decided on the policy,' which is how long the injunction lasts during the case, not a claim about later elections." chip="mislabeled" status="warn"><Cite s="hill-2026-09-06" passage="permit the administration to bar the U.S. Postal Service" /></OutletCheck>
          <OutletCheck outlet="Associated Press" claim={`wrote that Talwani extended an order against the Postal Service &ldquo;enforcing President Donald Trump's executive order.&rdquo; PBS NewsHour and CNBC carried the same dispatch.`} verdict="The Sunday filing asks a stay of the September 4 injunction of the Postal Service rule. The August 24 Supreme Court stay had already paused the injunction of the executive order. The third-time count, the Sauer quotes, and the North Carolina mailing line check out against 26A305." chip="mislabeled" status="warn"><Cite s="ap-2026-09-06" passage="enforcing President Donald Trump" /><Cite s="pbs-2026-09-06" passage="enforcing President Donald Trump" /></OutletCheck>
          <OutletCheck outlet="CBS News" claim={`wrote that Talwani extended an order against the Postal Service &ldquo;enforcing President Trump's executive order.&rdquo;`} verdict="Same mismatch as the Associated Press dispatch: the September 4 injunction names sections of the Postal Service rule, not the March executive order. CBS did not write 'Donald' in that line." chip="mislabeled" status="warn"><Cite s="cbs-2026-09-06" passage="enforcing President Trump's executive order" /></OutletCheck>
          <OutletCheck outlet="NBC News" claim={`wrote that the rule would let the Postal Service &ldquo;potentially reject ballots that are sent to and from voters not included in a federal database.&rdquo;`} verdict="That wording checks out. USPS 820.225 is a federal Privacy Act system at Postal Service headquarters and a supplier cloud environment. It records names, addresses, issuing states, and barcodes, keeps them for five years, and states a law-enforcement purpose. State and local officials load the rows. It is not a citizenship file." chip="checks out" status="ok"><Cite s="nbc-2026-09-06" passage="not included in a federal database" /><Cite s="usps-sorn-820-225" passage="Records are retained for 5 years." /></OutletCheck>
          <OutletCheck outlet="NPR" claim={`wrote on September 3 that key parts of the USPS plan were blocked by a temporary restraining order, that voting by mail was still available to eligible voters, and that the August ruling was on a procedural question.`} verdict="Checks out against the August 27 order then in force, 26A124's ripeness holding, and the September 3 docket." chip="checks out" status="ok"><Cite s="npr-2026-09-03" passage="currently blocked by a court order" /></OutletCheck>
          <OutletCheck outlet="The Hill" claim={`wrote on September 3 that the Court had lifted the judge's block on the executive order itself, and that she had now blocked the Postal Service's final plan.`} verdict="Consistent with 26A124 and the August 27 temporary order then before the Court." chip="consistent" status="ok"><Cite s="hill-2026-09-03" passage="The high court previously lifted the judge" /></OutletCheck>
        </div>
      </section>

      <section id="claim-check">
        <p class="sec-kicker" data-layer="narrative">What Sunday's application actually asks</p>
        <h2 data-layer="fact">Claim checked against the record</h2>

        <div data-layer="fact">
          <CheckedBlock label="The coverage's claim: the administration is asking the Court to let it enforce the March executive order for the midterms" variant="contested">
            <p>Too broad. Application 26A305 asks a stay of the September 4 injunction of named sections of the Postal Service rule issued August 21 and published August 26. The March order's own injunction was stayed on August 24, and that opinion said the stay did not decide whether later implementing measures would be lawful. The injunction the Court is being asked to lift reaches mandatory outbound envelope standards and review; mandatory return-envelope standards and review; mandatory portal registration; mandatory voter enrollment; outbound verification; the first two sentences of the presentation rules; the nonacceptance, further-review, and no-responsibility clauses; and further preparatory steps to implement those sections.<Cite s="scotus-26a305-application" passage="APPLICATION FOR A STAY OF THE INJUNCTION" /><Cite s="talwani-pi-2026-09-04" passage="may not take any further preparatory steps to implement" /></p>
          </CheckedBlock>
        </div>

        <p data-layer="narrative">The claim readers brought is the one in the candidate headlines: a new block, and another appeal. Both are true as dates. The new block is the September 4 injunction. The earlier appeal that "another" points to is 26A297 on Thursday, and behind that 26A124 in July.<Cite s="scotus-26a297-docket" passage="Application (26A297) withdrawn." /></p>

        <details class="receipt" data-layer="proof"><summary>how the day counts were made</summary>
          <p>November 3, 2026, minus September 6 is {computed.daysFilingToElection} days. Minus September 4 is {computed.daysPiToElection}. Minus September 3 is {computed.days297ToElection}. Minus August 26 (publication) is {computed.daysRuleToElection}. Minus August 21 (effective date, rule line 73) is {computed.daysEffectiveToElection}. Minus March 31 is {computed.daysEoToElection}. September 19 minus September 6 is {computed.daysFilingToUocava}. November 3 minus September 19 is {computed.uocavaDaysBeforeElection}, the statutory 45-day overseas and military ballot date when a valid request arrived in time; the rule excludes UOCAVA ballots. August 26 minus August 24 is {computed.daysStayToRule}. August 24 minus August 21 is {computed.daysEffectiveToStay}. Plaintiff states were counted from the parties list in 26A305: {computed.plaintiffStates} named states, plus the District of Columbia and the Pennsylvania governor, and {computed.intervenorStates} named intervenors.</p>
        </details>
      </section>

      <section id="what-happened-next">
        <p class="sec-kicker" data-layer="narrative">After Sunday</p>
        <h2 data-layer="fact">What happened next</h2>

        <p data-layer="narrative">On September 8 the 26A305 docket added three amicus briefs: from bipartisan current and former members of Congress, from the Society for the Rule of Law, and, in the evening, from Whistleblower Aid. The Congress brief argues that the order and final rule usurp Congress's constitutional role as the ultimate authority on federal-election administration. The Whistleblower Aid brief puts the disclosure's account of batch rejection and sampling before the justices. Jackson's response deadline remains 4 p.m. on September 9; as of Monday evening the docket showed no response and no stay order.<Cite s="scotus-26a305-congress-amicus" passage="THE ORDER AND FINAL RULE USURP CONGRESS" /><Cite s="scotus-26a305-docket-2026-09-08" passage="Amicus brief of Whistleblower Aid submitted." /><Cite s="scotus-26a305-docket-2026-09-08" passage="Amicus brief of The Society for The Rule of Law submitted." /></p>

        <p data-layer="narrative">Watching: the September 9 responses, any administrative stay, and whether Alabama mails on the date Sauer named.<Cite s="scotus-26a305-application" passage="Alabama" /></p>
      </section>

      <section id="unknowns">
        <p class="sec-kicker" data-layer="narrative">Open questions</p>
        <h2 data-layer="fact">What we do not know yet</h2>
        <ul data-layer="fact">
          <li>Whether respondents filed on September 9, and whether any administrative stay or full stay has issued. The docket we hold shows Jackson's 4 p.m. Eastern deadline, not a response or a stay order.<Cite s="scotus-26a305-docket" passage="4 p.m. (EDT) on September 9, 2026." /></li>
          <li>The official First Circuit dockets in Nos. 26-2029 and 26-2031, including any September 7 filings or order. The Sunday application lists stay motions filed September 5 in those numbers. The Circuit's order is not in our records.<Cite s="scotus-26a305-application" passage="No. 26-2031" /></li>
          <li>Whether the Postal Service produced the records Senator Blumenthal asked for by September 8. His letter set that date; we do not hold a production.<Cite s="blumenthal-whistleblower-2026-09-01" passage="by September 8, 2026" /></li>
          <li>Which five states Sauer meant by "the week of September 13" in the Sunday application. The Thursday application named Indiana, Florida, Michigan, Nevada, and Wisconsin among "at least 16 more States" if the temporary order were extended.<Cite s="scotus-26a305-application" passage="the week of September 13" /><Cite s="scotus-26a297-application" passage="including Indiana, Florida," /></li>
          <li>How many mail ballots each plaintiff state has already sent. Sauer names North Carolina as having begun; the application does not give a count. The state forecasts above are forecasts.<Cite s="scotus-26a305-application" passage="Ballots have already begun to be mailed in North Carolina" /></li>
          <li>Whether the portal works. The whistleblower's lawyers say the systems were "still inoperable" at the September hearing; the Postmaster General says testing was continuous. No test report, defect list, or sampling specification is on any docket we hold.<Cite s="scotus-26a305-whistleblower-aid-amicus" passage="still inoperable" /><Cite s="usps-steiner-statement-2026-09-04" passage="Testing and validation have been continuous" /></li>
          <li>The Society for the Rule of Law brief itself. The docket lists it; we hold the entry, not the brief.<Cite s="scotus-26a305-docket-2026-09-08" passage="Amicus brief of The Society for The Rule of Law submitted." /></li>
        </ul>
      </section>

